From 3fd66df960cce139cff88bcfe2ee15785c387230 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Un1box <un1box@localhost>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 20:26:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm: the frontend's microphone into a capture port

The guest's capture path is already here and already checked: CDMA 0x1b reads
i2s0's RX FIFO, and on the iPad it recorded a 1000 Hz tone at 44025 frames/s
with no discontinuities, and Shazam recorded through it. What it reads comes
from a QEMU input voice, and on a phone there is none: the engine runs its
audio backend as `driver=none`, so `audio_be_open_in` returns nothing and a
recording app in the guest hears silence.

This is the input counterpart of the output capture tap the portable library
already installs, with the same shape: the frontend pushes what its microphone
recorded into a bounded ring, and the capture port hands it to the guest
instead of silence. The app records at 44100 (what iOS uses, and what
AudioRecord opens at), the port reads at its own frame rate, and a 16.16
accumulator converts between them, nearest neighbour: exact at 44.1 kHz,
pitch-correct at any other rate.

The ring has its own mutex, never blocks either side, and holds 4096 stereo
frames (93 ms); a full ring drops the app's block, and an app that has been
quiet for 250 ms gives the port silence rather than replaying its tail. A
stopped capture flushes, so a recording's unread frames cannot turn up at the
head of the next one.

The ring is asked BEFORE the port's own input voice, and that ordering is the
difference between a working capture and silence. `driver=none` does open an
input voice -- audio/noaudio.c declares max_voices_in, initializes it, and
no_read() returns silence paced at the port's rate -- so a ring placed behind it
as a fallback is never reached and the guest records silence. host_mic_read_frame()
returns false when the frontend pushed nothing, which is every case on the Mac
and every case with the Android switch off; only then does the voice's ring
answer, exactly as before.

Nothing about the guest's side changes: the same RX FIFO, the same CDMA chain,
the same pacing. The test-only `tone-hz` property still wins over both.

  hw/arm/host-mic.c, include/hw/arm/host-mic.h   the ring, its resampler, counters
  hw/arm/s5l8930_i2s.c                           the RX FIFO reads it, the port's rate is handed to it
  contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c/.h       qemu_ios_microphone_write/flush/state (API 2.4)
  hw/arm/meson.build                             host-mic.c for the S5L8920/S5L8930 family

The boards that use this model are the ones with a microphone in the hardware:
iPhone 3GS, iPhone 4, iPad 1 -- and the iPod touch 4G, whose iOS 6 may still
offer input. The iPod touch 2G and 3G have no microphone and keep their own I2S
model, which hands out silence.
